# ==========================================================================
#  Q108  When the other end is not there
# ==========================================================================
#
#  ASKS
#    What happens when the remote endpoint is unreachable, and why does
#    HOLOS refuse to call one at all?
#
#  HOW IT WORKS
#    SERVICE SILENT tells the engine to carry on with no bindings rather
#    than fail when a remote call goes wrong. Fuseki honours it. HOLOS --
#    and so the browser editor, which now runs it -- declines to make the
#    request in the first place, and its reason is worth understanding.
#    The editor's previous engine raised instead, honouring neither.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#        SERVICE SILENT <https://endpoint.invalid/sparql> { ... }
#
#        measured against a host that does not exist:
#
#          +---------+------------------+--------------------------+
#          |         | SERVICE          | SERVICE SILENT           |
#          +---------+------------------+--------------------------+
#          | Fuseki  | error            | 3 rows, no bindings   ok |
#          | editor  | error            | error                    |
#          | HOLOS   | refuses          | refuses                  |
#          +---------+------------------+--------------------------+
#
#        WHY HOLOS REFUSES
#
#        A SERVICE IRI is a URL chosen by whoever wrote the query. An
#        engine that follows it will make its server issue a request to
#        that address -- from inside your network:
#
#            SERVICE <http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/>
#            SERVICE <http://localhost:9200/>
#            SERVICE <http://admin.internal/>
#
#        That is server-side request forgery, and the query language is
#        the attack surface. HOLOS evaluates SERVICE only against
#        endpoints registered in the process, and refuses remote HTTP
#        outright; it refuses remote LOAD for the same reason. Enabling
#        it safely needs an allow-list, which is a policy decision rather
#        than a default.
#
#        Not a bug, then, but a position. Worth knowing which position
#        your own endpoint takes before you expose it.
#
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - SERVICE SILENT continues with no bindings instead of failing.
#      Fuseki honours it. The browser editor refuses the remote call and
#      then leaves the local rows standing, which is SILENT doing its job
#      over a request that was never made.
#    - A public endpoint that follows arbitrary SERVICE IRIs will make
#      requests to any address a stranger names, including ones only it
#      can reach. That is SSRF, and it is why HOLOS refuses.
#    - Federation is a network operation wearing the clothes of a join:
#      it can be slow, it can fail, and it can be a security question.
#
#  NOTE
#    Fuseki is the one that actually calls out and shrugs the failure off.
#    The browser editor now runs the HOLOS engine, so it behaves as HOLOS
#    does: it refuses remote SERVICE altogether and SILENT then leaves the
#    local rows standing -- five shops, no population column. The Comunica
#    build it used before raised instead, honouring neither the call nor
#    the SILENT. Refusing is the point of the query rather than a
#    limitation of it; raising was simply wrong.
#
#  NETWORK  calls a remote endpoint; needs the internet
#  DATA     bookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  LOAD IT  https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FSPARQL_Course%2Fmain%2Fdata%2Fbookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  RUNS ON  fuseki
#  SPEC     SPARQL 1.2 Query, Security Considerations
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#c-security-considerations
#           SPARQL 1.2 Query 13. RDF Dataset
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x13-rdf-dataset
#  RETURNS  5 rows, on every engine that runs it
# ==========================================================================

PREFIX bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>

SELECT ?name ?remote
WHERE {
  ?shop a          bs:Bookshop ;
        rdfs:label ?name .

  # The host does not exist. SILENT is what decides whether that
  # ends the query or is simply shrugged off.
  SERVICE SILENT <https://endpoint.invalid/sparql> {
    ?shop bs:somethingRemote ?remote .
  }
}
ORDER BY ?name
LIMIT 5
